[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6, with Be7/Bb4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b0d2d4e7-3982-4eae-a6fe-2c2daabb8e08/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black replies with 1...e6 - this is the third most popular response to 1. e4. The idea is quite simple - the move e7-e6 supports the advance d7-d5. We'll go for an interesting sideline with b2-b3. French players are often well-prepared in the main lines - in this sideline we hope either: (1) They think we're terrible and take the pawn/hope for the best. (2) They get too scared of our gambit and play too passively.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways... The move c7-c5 would now transpose to the b3 Sicilian with 2...e6 - refer to the course on the b3 Sicilian!} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black defends the e-pawn by developing the kingside knight to f6. We move our queen over to e2 and add an attacker to to the e-pawn. Black now has options...} 5.Qe2 5...Be7 {The most common is 5...Be7 - Black develops the dark-squared bishop and prepares to castle kingside. Don't rush to recapture! We should proceed by castling queenside.} 6.O-O-O 6...O-O {Black castles kingside. We still don't take on e4! Instead, we'll kickstart a kingside attack with g2-g4 and are looking to dislodge Black's knight by pushing the pawn up to g5. A useful rule of thumb is that, in all of these positions, a7-a5 should be met with a2-a4.} 7.g4 7...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight to c6 - this is the most common. We'll attack Black's knight by pushing the g-pawn up to g5!} 8.g5 8...Nd4 {Black counterattacks our queen by jumping their knight into d4. This is the critical move, and the crazy ensuing sequence of moves is forced. We'll slide our queen up to e3.} 9.Qe3 9...Ng4 {Black attacks our queen once again by jumping their knight into g4. Our queen needs to keep an eye on f2, so we'll move it to g3 and attack the marauding knight.} 10.Qg3 10...f5 {Black plays the only move, f7-f5! We'll attack Black's knight once more with h2-h3.} 11.h3 11...Bd6 {Black counterattacks our queen by moving their bishop to d6. We'll block the attack with f2-f4!} 12.f4 12...exf3 {Black captures en passant and opens up an attack on our queen. We'll move our queen out to h4. The g4-knight is still under attack and Black can't simply move it since the d4-knight would then be en prise.} 13.Qh4 13...Ne2+ {So Black jumps the knight into e2 and gives a check to our king! This is the only move that doesn't lose material. We'll capture on e2 with our c-knight. This is important since we want to open the long diagonal for our b2-bishop.} 14.Ncxe2 14...fxe2 {Black recaptures on e2 with the pawn. We'll capture on g4 - threatening the devastating Qxh7+!} 15.hxg4 15...exd1=Q+ {Black captures our rook on d1 and makes a queen with check! We'll recapture on d1 - this is the only legal move.} 16.Kxd1 16...h6 {Black prevents Qxh7+ with h7-h6. This is, once again, the only move that doesn't lose.} 17.Nf3 {We develop our knight to f3. This move might seem a little slow, but we need to prevent Qxg5. The computer only gives a slight edge for White, but this position is incredibly difficult to defend from a human standpoint. We're down an exchange, but we have a raging kingside attack and two of Black's pieces aren't even playing. One of our main motifs is Bxg7!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6, with Be7/Bb4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b0d2d4e7-3982-4eae-a6fe-2c2daabb8e08/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black replies with 1...e6 - this is the third most popular response to 1. e4. The idea is quite simple - the move e7-e6 supports the advance d7-d5. We'll go for an interesting sideline with b2-b3. French players are often well-prepared in the main lines - in this sideline we hope either: (1) They think we're terrible and take the pawn/hope for the best. (2) They get too scared of our gambit and play too passively.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways... The move c7-c5 would now transpose to the b3 Sicilian with 2...e6 - refer to the course on the b3 Sicilian!} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black defends the e-pawn by developing the kingside knight to f6. We move our queen over to e2 and add an attacker to to the e-pawn. Black now has options...} 5.Qe2 5...Be7 {The most common is 5...Be7 - Black develops the dark-squared bishop and prepares to castle kingside. Don't rush to recapture! We should proceed by castling queenside.} 6.O-O-O 6...O-O {Black castles kingside. We still don't take on e4! Instead, we'll kickstart a kingside attack with g2-g4 and are looking to dislodge Black's knight by pushing the pawn up to g5. A useful rule of thumb is that, in all of these positions, a7-a5 should be met with a2-a4.} 7.g4 7...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight to c6 - this is the most common. We'll attack Black's knight by pushing the g-pawn up to g5!} 8.g5 8...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5, opening up an attack on the g-pawn.} 9.h4 {We'll support the g-pawn with h2-h4. White now has a slight objective edge and great attacking prospects on the kingside. We will look to recapture the d-pawn with our queen, and our f1-bishop can (but doesn't have to) develop to g2. In all of these b3 French positions, any Nxc3 should be met with dxc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6, with Be7/Bb4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b0d2d4e7-3982-4eae-a6fe-2c2daabb8e08/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black replies with 1...e6 - this is the third most popular response to 1. e4. The idea is quite simple - the move e7-e6 supports the advance d7-d5. We'll go for an interesting sideline with b2-b3. French players are often well-prepared in the main lines - in this sideline we hope either: (1) They think we're terrible and take the pawn/hope for the best. (2) They get too scared of our gambit and play too passively.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways... The move c7-c5 would now transpose to the b3 Sicilian with 2...e6 - refer to the course on the b3 Sicilian!} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black defends the e-pawn by developing the kingside knight to f6. We move our queen over to e2 and add an attacker to to the e-pawn. Black now has options...} 5.Qe2 5...Be7 {The most common is 5...Be7 - Black develops the dark-squared bishop and prepares to castle kingside. Don't rush to recapture! We should proceed by castling queenside.} 6.O-O-O 6...O-O {Black castles kingside. We still don't take on e4! Instead, we'll kickstart a kingside attack with g2-g4 and are looking to dislodge Black's knight by pushing the pawn up to g5. A useful rule of thumb is that, in all of these positions, a7-a5 should be met with a2-a4.} 7.g4 7...e5 {Black pushes e5 and opens up an attack on the g4-pawn. We'll attack Black's knight by pushing the g-pawn up to g5! Black now has two options...} 8.g5 8...Bg4 {Black counterattacks our queen by developing the bishop to g4. Our rook and queen are skewered, so we will have to interpose with f2-f3.} 9.f3 9...exf3 {Black captures on f3. We'll recapture with the knight.} 10.Nxf3 10...Nd5 {Black's knight was under attack, so they jump it into d5. If Black had tried pushing e4 instead, we would simply win material by capturing on f6.} 11.Rg1 {We attack Black's bishop with Rg1. With the more active pieces, and with great attacking prospects on the kingside, White is now simply better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6, with Be7/Bb4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b0d2d4e7-3982-4eae-a6fe-2c2daabb8e08/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black replies with 1...e6 - this is the third most popular response to 1. e4. The idea is quite simple - the move e7-e6 supports the advance d7-d5. We'll go for an interesting sideline with b2-b3. French players are often well-prepared in the main lines - in this sideline we hope either: (1) They think we're terrible and take the pawn/hope for the best. (2) They get too scared of our gambit and play too passively.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways... The move c7-c5 would now transpose to the b3 Sicilian with 2...e6 - refer to the course on the b3 Sicilian!} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black defends the e-pawn by developing the kingside knight to f6. We move our queen over to e2 and add an attacker to to the e-pawn. Black now has options...} 5.Qe2 5...Bb4 {Black develops the bishop to b4. The main idea of this move is to prevent us from recapturing the pawn on e4. We'll proceed by castling queenside. If Black replies by castling kingside, we should simply recapture on e4.} 6.O-O-O 6...Qe7 {Black moves the queen over to e7 and is looking to follow up with Ba3. We'll push g2-g4 and are looking to dislodge Black's knight by pushing the pawn up to g5.} 7.g4 7...Ba3 {Black follows up with Ba3. The main idea is to trade the dark-squared bishops and weaken the dark squares around our king. We'll attack Black's knight by pushing the g-pawn up to g5.} 8.g5 8...Bxb2+ {Black trades bishops on b2. We'll recapture.} 9.Kxb2 9...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5, opening up an attack on the g-pawn.} 10.h4 {We support the g-pawn with h2-h4. The position is now close to equality, but White certainly has an easier game and great attacking prospects in the middlegame. We should look to capture the e-pawn (preferably with our queen), and the f1-bishop can develop to g2.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6, with Be7/Bb4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b0d2d4e7-3982-4eae-a6fe-2c2daabb8e08/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black replies with 1...e6 - this is the third most popular response to 1. e4. The idea is quite simple - the move e7-e6 supports the advance d7-d5. We'll go for an interesting sideline with b2-b3. French players are often well-prepared in the main lines - in this sideline we hope either: (1) They think we're terrible and take the pawn/hope for the best. (2) They get too scared of our gambit and play too passively.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways... The move c7-c5 would now transpose to the b3 Sicilian with 2...e6 - refer to the course on the b3 Sicilian!} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black defends the e-pawn by developing the kingside knight to f6. We move our queen over to e2 and add an attacker to to the e-pawn. Black now has options...} 5.Qe2 5...Bb4 {Black develops the bishop to b4. The main idea of this move is to prevent us from recapturing the pawn on e4. We'll proceed by castling queenside. If Black replies by castling kingside, we should simply recapture on e4.} 6.O-O-O 6...Bxc3 {Our knight was attacking the e-pawn, so Black chooses to remove it by capturing on c3. We'll recapture with the d-pawn, opening up the d-file for our rook.} 7.dxc3 7...Qe7 {Black slides the queen over to e7.} 8.g4 {We kickstart an attack on the kingside with g2-g4! White now has a slight objective edge, and great attacking prospects in the middlegame. We can look to dislodge Black's knight with g5, and support g5 with h2-h4. We should also look to open up our dark-squared bishop with c4, and our f1-bishop can develop to g2.} *

