[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 3: Black Plays 4...f5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b24b8de1-a748-4836-8fc3-f4e2cee0d44e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The French Defense. We will fianchetto.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways...} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...f5 {Black supports the e-pawn with f7-f5. We push d2-d3, looking to chip away at Black's central structure.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black doesn't capture on d3 and instead develops the kingside knight. We'll proceed by capturing on e4.} 6.dxe4 6...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We'll recapture with the rook.} 7.Rxd1 7...Nxe4 {Black captures on e4 with the knight. We'll capture Black's knight on e4.} 8.Nxe4 8...fxe4 {Black recaptures on e4.} 9.Rd4 {We continue Rd4, looking to capture the e-pawn. White now has a pleasant edge and (after Rxe4) can continue with Bd3/Bb5, Nf3 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 3: Black Plays 4...f5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b24b8de1-a748-4836-8fc3-f4e2cee0d44e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The French Defense. We will fianchetto.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways...} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...f5 {Black supports the e-pawn with f7-f5. We push d2-d3, looking to chip away at Black's central structure.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black doesn't capture on d3 and instead develops the kingside knight. We'll proceed by capturing on e4.} 6.dxe4 6...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We'll recapture with the rook.} 7.Rxd1 7...fxe4 {Black captures on e4 with the f-pawn.} 8.Nh3 {We continue Nh3, looking to jump our knight into g5 or f4! The evaluation of this position is close to equality, but White retains great practical chances with the superior piece activity and Black's weak pawns on the e-file. We will look to continue with Ng5/Nf4, Bc4 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Accepts The Gambit / Study 3: Black Plays 4...f5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/71582daa-ad76-444e-a615-6ece4eaa9040/studies/b24b8de1-a748-4836-8fc3-f4e2cee0d44e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The French Defense. We will fianchetto.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! Black can now accept the gambit or decline it in a variety of ways...} 3.Bb2 3...dxe4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing the e4-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and attack the e4-pawn. Our plan is Nc3/Qe2/0-0-0/g4, in various orders.} 4.Nc3 4...f5 {Black supports the e-pawn with f7-f5. We push d2-d3, looking to chip away at Black's central structure.} 5.d3 5...exd3 {Black captures on d3. We'll recapture with the bishop.} 6.Bxd3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight.} 7.Qe2 {We move our queen to e2, pinning the e6-pawn and preparing O-O-O. White objectively better, and the position is very pleasant. The immediate threat is Bxf5, and Black will struggle to defend against the pressure which will be built up with Nf3, O-O-O & Bc4. We should look to meet Bb4 with a3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Doesn't Play Into The Gambit / Study 1: Black Declines With 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/063321ec-186f-4628-bf9a-94b47e0d290e/studies/09ffdd7f-1350-4d6b-9765-001bb2d333f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black plays the French Defense. We will go for our fianchetto idea.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! We are going to look at a couple of ways of declining the gambit.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black declines the gambit by developing the kingside knight. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight on f6!} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black retreats the knight to d7. We'll proceed by developing our queen out to g4. We place the queen there to limit the movement of Black's dark-squared bishop. Black is generally forced to eventually play g7-g6. Then our idea is to get the queen out of the way and advance the g- and h- pawns.} 5.Qg4 5...c5 {Black continues in typical French style by pushing the c-pawn and taking some central space. We'll push f2-f4, supporting the e5 pawn and preparing to develop the g1-knight behind the f-pawn.} 6.f4 6...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. We'll develop the kingside knight. Black can continue in a variety of ways, but our general plan is Be2 and O-O.} 7.Nf3 7...g6 {Black plays g7-g6, preparing to fianchetto the f8-bishop. We'll prepare to castle kingside by developing the f1-bishop to e2. If Black now attacks our queen with h2-h4, we should retreat it to g3.} 8.Be2 8...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We develop the queenside knight. We are now looking to jump the knight into b5, from where it will be able to access the d6 outpost. The move d4 is never an issue, since it gives us the important e4 square.} 9.Nc3 9...a6 {Black plays a7-a6, preventing Nb5.} 10.a4 {We continue a2-a4, preventing Black from easily expanding with b7-b5. White now has a slight edge, and we will look to proceed by castling short. The arising middlegame is very rich. Some typical ideas include h2-h4 and the maneuver Nd1-Ne3/Nf2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Doesn't Play Into The Gambit / Study 1: Black Declines With 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/063321ec-186f-4628-bf9a-94b47e0d290e/studies/09ffdd7f-1350-4d6b-9765-001bb2d333f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black plays the French Defense. We will go for our fianchetto idea.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! We are going to look at a couple of ways of declining the gambit.} 3.Bb2 3...d4 {Black declines the gambit by pushing the d-pawn to d4. This is not a great move, since we can target the pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's d-pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {Black supports the d-pawn with c7-c5. Instead, 4...Nc6 would allow us to play 5.Bb5 and win the d4-pawn. We'll push c2-c3, creating tension between the pawns and threatening to capture the pawn on d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and further supports the d4-pawn. 5...dxc3 would merely leave us with a big lead in development after 6.Nxc3. After 5...Nc6, we'll develop our light-squared bishop on b5, pinning the knight. Not only are we threatening to win a pawn with cxd4, but we also threaten to double Black's c-pawns with Bxc6.} 6.Bb5 6...dxc3 {Black attempts to solve their problems by capturing on c3 and releasing the tension. We'll capture on c6, forcing Black to double their c-pawns!} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 8.dxc3 {We recapture on c3 with the d-pawn. White is now simply better - we have a lead in development, a great bishop on b2, and Black's queenside pawn structure is damaged beyond repair.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Doesn't Play Into The Gambit / Study 1: Black Declines With 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/063321ec-186f-4628-bf9a-94b47e0d290e/studies/09ffdd7f-1350-4d6b-9765-001bb2d333f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black plays the French Defense. We will go for our fianchetto idea.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! We are going to look at a couple of ways of declining the gambit.} 3.Bb2 3...d4 {Black declines the gambit by pushing the d-pawn to d4. This is not a great move, since we can target the pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's d-pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {Black supports the d-pawn with c7-c5. Instead, 4...Nc6 would allow us to play 5.Bb5 and win the d4-pawn. We'll push c2-c3, creating tension between the pawns and threatening to capture the pawn on d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and further supports the d4-pawn. 5...dxc3 would merely leave us with a big lead in development after 6.Nxc3. After 5...Nc6, we'll develop our light-squared bishop on b5, pinning the knight. Not only are we threatening to win a pawn with cxd4, but we also threaten to double Black's c-pawns with Bxc6.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {Black supports and unpins the knight by moving their bishop to d7. We'll move our queen to e2. This move might not make too much sense at first glance, but it is important that we support the bishop on b5.} 7.Qe2 7...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll capture the pawn on d4.} 8.cxd4 8...cxd4 {Black recaptures on d4. We capture on d4 with the knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...Nxd4 {Black captures our knight on d4.} 10.Bxd4 {We capture Black's knight on d4. The idea of Qe2 is now apparent - if our queen wasn't on e2, Black would have been able to capture the b5-bishop! White now simply has an extra pawn, and should look to continue with O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Doesn't Play Into The Gambit / Study 1: Black Declines With 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/063321ec-186f-4628-bf9a-94b47e0d290e/studies/09ffdd7f-1350-4d6b-9765-001bb2d333f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black plays the French Defense. We will go for our fianchetto idea.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! We are going to look at a couple of ways of declining the gambit.} 3.Bb2 3...d4 {Black declines the gambit by pushing the d-pawn to d4. This is not a great move, since we can target the pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight and attack Black's d-pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {Black supports the d-pawn with c7-c5. Instead, 4...Nc6 would allow us to play 5.Bb5 and win the d4-pawn. We'll push c2-c3, creating tension between the pawns and threatening to capture the pawn on d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and further supports the d4-pawn. 5...dxc3 would merely leave us with a big lead in development after 6.Nxc3. After 5...Nc6, we'll develop our light-squared bishop on b5, pinning the knight. Not only are we threatening to win a pawn with cxd4, but we also threaten to double Black's c-pawns with Bxc6.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {Black supports and unpins the knight by moving their bishop to d7. We'll move our queen to e2. This move might not make too much sense at first glance, but it is important that we support the bishop on b5.} 7.Qe2 7...dxc3 {Black captures the pawn on c3.} 8.dxc3 {We recapture on c3 with the d-pawn. This is probably the best Black can get, but White still has an edge and White's position is easier to play. We'll continue with Nbd2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Doesn't Play Into The Gambit / Study 1: Black Declines With 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/063321ec-186f-4628-bf9a-94b47e0d290e/studies/09ffdd7f-1350-4d6b-9765-001bb2d333f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black plays the French Defense. We will go for our fianchetto idea.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We fianchetto our bishop, offering up the e-pawn for capture! We are going to look at a couple of ways of declining the gambit.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black declines the gambit by developing the kingside knight. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight on f6!} 4.e5 4...Ne4 {Black might also jump the knight into e4, but this isn't great since the knight simply becomes a target. We'll attack Black's knight with d2-d3!} 5.d3 5...Nc5 {Black jumps the knight to c5. We'll push d4 and attack Black's knight once more! If Black now jumps the knight back to e4, we should play for g3 and f3. It is important to play g3 before f3 in order to prevent Black from playing Qh4+.} 6.d4 6...Ncd7 {Black retreats the knight to d7.} 7.Bd3 {We develop our bishop to d3. Black moved the same piece four times in the opening - White now simply has a space advantage and a huge lead in development. We should look to continue developing with c2-c3, Nge2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Owen's Defense (e6/b6 Setup) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6/Bb4/c5/d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/d8b77ec5-c52a-4ae8-8e4c-108a9ec8db7e/studies/b43db86a-95fe-48df-b48f-56e3ca397d9a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The start of the French Defense. We'll go for our fianchetto!} 2.b3 2...b6 {Black chooses not to advance with d5, and instead sets up with e6 and b6. We'll simply fianchetto our bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop and attacks our e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing the queenside knight to c3. After this we have to consider a few handful of options for Black.} 4.Nc3 4...c5 {Black pushes c7-c5, turning the position into an unusual Sicilian. We'll strike in the center by pushing our d-pawn two squares.} 5.d4 5...cxd4 {Black captures on d4. We recapture on d4 with the queen.} 6.Qxd4 6...Nc6 {Black develops their queenside knight and attacks our queen.} 7.Qd2 {We retreat the queen to e2. White now has a slight edge and great attacking prospects on the kingside. We'll look to continue with O-O-O, Nge2 and f2-f3, and we can launch a kingside attack with h2-h4 and g2-g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Owen's Defense (e6/b6 Setup) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6/Bb4/c5/d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/d8b77ec5-c52a-4ae8-8e4c-108a9ec8db7e/studies/b43db86a-95fe-48df-b48f-56e3ca397d9a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The start of the French Defense. We'll go for our fianchetto!} 2.b3 2...b6 {Black chooses not to advance with d5, and instead sets up with e6 and b6. We'll simply fianchetto our bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop and attacks our e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing the queenside knight to c3. After this we have to consider a few handful of options for Black.} 4.Nc3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the bishop out to b4. The idea of this move is to remove the only defender of the e4-pawn. We'll move our queen out to g4 and attack the g7-pawn!} 5.Qg4 5...Qf6 {Black moves the queen out to f6 and defends the g-pawn - this is the most natural and most common move here. We'll proceed by castling queenside. We are now threatening to win on the spot with the crushing Nd5! If Black now attacks our queen with Nh6, we should retreat it to e2.} 6.O-O-O 6...Qg6 {Black slides the queen over to g6 and offers a queen trade. We'll oblige and trade queens on g6.} 7.Qxg6 7...hxg6 {Black recaptures on g6, opening the h-file for their rook. We'll jump our knight into b5. This move threatens Nxc7+ and opens up an attack on the g7-pawn!} 8.Nb5 8...Na6 {Black defends the c7 square by moving the knight out to a6. But now we can capture the pawn on g7!} 9.Bxg7 9...Rh5 {Black moves the rook up to h5.} 10.Bc3 {We retreat the bishop to c3 - this is the only move that keeps the extra pawn! We should look to continue by supporting the e-pawn with f2-f3 as soon as possible. Notable is that we can simply meet Bxe4 with Bxb4 and Nxc7+.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Owen's Defense (e6/b6 Setup) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6/Bb4/c5/d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/d8b77ec5-c52a-4ae8-8e4c-108a9ec8db7e/studies/b43db86a-95fe-48df-b48f-56e3ca397d9a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The start of the French Defense. We'll go for our fianchetto!} 2.b3 2...b6 {Black chooses not to advance with d5, and instead sets up with e6 and b6. We'll simply fianchetto our bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop and attacks our e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing the queenside knight to c3. After this we have to consider a few handful of options for Black.} 4.Nc3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the bishop out to b4. The idea of this move is to remove the only defender of the e4-pawn. We'll move our queen out to g4 and attack the g7-pawn!} 5.Qg4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and attacks our queen. This move gives up the g7 pawn, but it is the engine's top choice. We'll capture the pawn on g7.} 6.Qxg7 6...Rg8 {Black moves the rook to g8 and attacks our queen. We'll move our queen to the only safe square, h6. If Black now proceeds with Rg6, we can simply retreat the queen to e3 and defend our e4-pawn.} 7.Qh6 7...Nxe4 {Black captures the pawn on e4. We'll capture the pawn on h7 and attack Black's rook.} 8.Qxh7 8...Nf6 {Black defends the rook and attacks our queen by retreating their knight to f6. We slide our queen back to h4 and attack Black's bishop on b4.} 9.Qh4 9...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7.} 10.O-O-O {We proceed by castling queenside. The immediate Ng4 should now be met with Qh7, and we can meet a subsequent Nf6 with Qh3, holding onto g2. We are a pawn up, and the onus is on Black to demonstrate that they have any compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Owen's Defense (e6/b6 Setup) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6/Bb4/c5/d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/d8b77ec5-c52a-4ae8-8e4c-108a9ec8db7e/studies/b43db86a-95fe-48df-b48f-56e3ca397d9a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The start of the French Defense. We'll go for our fianchetto!} 2.b3 2...b6 {Black chooses not to advance with d5, and instead sets up with e6 and b6. We'll simply fianchetto our bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop and attacks our e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing the queenside knight to c3. After this we have to consider a few handful of options for Black.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and adds an attacker to the e4-pawn. We'll push e5 and attack Black's knight!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll exchange it off.} 6.Nxd5 6...Bxd5 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll push d4, expanding in the center and supporting the e5-pawn. If Black now tries to undermine our pawns with ...c5, we should expand further with c4 and d5!} 7.d4 7...Be7 {Black develops their dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O.} 8.Nh3 {We develop our knight out to h3 and are looking to jump it into f4. White now has a comfortable objective edge and great attacking prospects on the kingside. We'll continue with Nf4 & Bd3, and can try to get Qg4 and O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Owen's Defense (e6/b6 Setup) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Nf6/Bb4/c5/d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d673407c-79dc-497c-9bb8-a2a709bf6df9/chapters/d8b77ec5-c52a-4ae8-8e4c-108a9ec8db7e/studies/b43db86a-95fe-48df-b48f-56e3ca397d9a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {The start of the French Defense. We'll go for our fianchetto!} 2.b3 2...b6 {Black chooses not to advance with d5, and instead sets up with e6 and b6. We'll simply fianchetto our bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop and attacks our e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing the queenside knight to c3. After this we have to consider a few handful of options for Black.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Black strikes in the center with d5. We'll respond by capturing the d-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...exd5 {Black recaptures. We'll prepare to castle queenside by developing our queen on f3.} 6.Qf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll proceed by castling queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O.} 8.g4 {Out of nowhere, we launch a kingside attack with g4! White now has a very comfortable edge with great attacking prospects on the kingside.} *